2.text:
In the $x-z$ plane put
\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=A(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{z}=B(z)\cos kx ,\qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that
\begin{equation*}
B'=-kA,\qquad 
kP+\mu\big(A''-k^{2}A\big)=0,
\qquad 
-P'+\mu\big(B''-k^{2}B\big)=0,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
A''''-2k^{2}A''+k^{4}A=0 ,
\qquad 
A(z)=\big(a_{0}+a_{1}z\big)e^{kz}+\big(-a_{0}+b_{1}z\big)
e^{-kz}.
\end{equation*}
The incompressibility condition gives
\begin{equation*}
kB=\frac{1}{k^{2}}A'''-2A' ,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big(A'-kB\big)\sin kx ,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=-\big(P+2\mu kA\big)\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
With $u_{x}=0$ on $z=0$,
$u_{z}=0$ on $z=0$ in the steady state and
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx , \qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0,
\end{equation*}
we have
\begin{equation*}
A(0)=0,\qquad B(0)=0,\qquad 
P(0)=-\rho ge,\qquad P(h)=-2\mu kA(h),
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
A''(0)=\frac{k\rho g}{\mu}e,\qquad 
A'''(0)=2k^{2}A'(0),\qquad 
A''(h)=3k^{2}A(h).
\end{equation*}
The basal traction is
\begin{equation*}
\mu\big(A'-kB\big)_{h}=S_{0},\qquad 
3A'(h)-\frac{1}{k^{2}}A'''(h)=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu} .
\end{equation*}
Putting
\begin{equation*}
a_{1}-k a_{0}=E,\qquad 
E=\frac{\rho g e}{2\mu},
\end{equation*}
the four conditions at $z=h$ give with $C=\cosh kh$, $S=\sinh kh$
\begin{equation*}
E\big[C-khS\big]=k^{2}hC\,a_{0},\qquad
a_{0}\big[C+khS\big]+hCE=\frac{S_{0}}{4k\mu},
\end{equation*}
whence
\begin{equation*}
a_{0}=\frac{S_{0}}{4k\mu}\,
\frac{C-khS}{C^{2}\!\left(1+k^{2}h^{2}\right)-k^{2}h^{2}S^{2}},
\qquad 
E=\frac{S_{0}khC}{4\mu\Big[\,C^{2}+k^{2}h^{2}\,\Big]} .
\end{equation*}
With $E=\rho ge/2\mu$,
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=
\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
\frac{kh\,\cosh kh}{2\Big[\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}\Big]}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the usual isostatic notation $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\chi\,
\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\,\cosh kh}
{2\big[\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}\big]}
      =\frac{kh\,\cosh kh}
      {2\cosh^{2}kh\big[1+(kh/\cosh kh)^{2}\big]}\;,
\end{equation*}
which with the notation of the statement is $e_{s}=
(\rho/\Delta\rho)\chi S_{0}/\rho g$.  In particular
$\chi\simeq kh/2$ for $kh\ll1$ and $\chi\sim kh\,e^{-kh}/2$ for
$kh\gg1$, so long wavelength basal tractions are the most efficient
in generating dynamic topography and short wavelengths are
exponentially damped.  If the $\rho$ in the normal stress condition is
identified with the density contrast $\Delta\rho$ the factor in front
of $S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ is the dimensionless number quoted above,
essentially $ \chi\approx kh\cosh kh/2[\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}]$,
and for the usual long-wave limit $e_{s}\simeq (kh/2)S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.
This is the steady viscous dynamic topography generated by the
harmonic basal shear. ∎

1.text:
For a stream function $\psi (x,z)=\Psi (z)\sin kx$ 

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Psi^{\prime}\sin kx\,,\qquad 
u_{z}=-k\Psi\cos kx\,,\qquad 
\Theta:=\Psi^{\prime\prime}-k^{2}\Psi 
\end{equation*}
the Stokes equations give $\Psi^{\rm{\rm IV}}-2k^{2}\Psi^{\prime\prime}+k^{4}\Psi=0$
i.e. 
\begin{equation*}
\Psi(z)=\big(a+bz\big)e^{kz}+\big(c+dz\big)e^{-kz}\,,
\end{equation*}
so that with the usual sign convention
\begin{equation*}
p=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Theta^{\prime}\cos kx\,,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=2\mu k\big[b e^{kz}+d e^{-kz}-\Psi^{\prime}\big]\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}=2\mu k\Big[b e^{kz}-d e^{-kz}\Big]\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
On the free surface $z=0$

\begin{equation*}
 u_{x}=0\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\Psi^{\prime}(0)=0 ,\qquad 
u_{z}= \dot e\cos kx\;, 
\end{equation*}
so that for a steady topography $\dot e=0$
\begin{equation*}
\Psi(0)=0\,,\qquad \Psi^{\prime}(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=2\mu k(b+d)\cos kx=\Delta\rho g\,e\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the sign convention used in the statement
\begin{equation*}
e(t)=\frac{2\mu k}{\Delta\rho g}\big[b+d\big] .
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
At the base $z=h$ 
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
2k\Big[b e^{kh}-d e^{-kh}\Big]=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu},\qquad 
\Psi^{\prime}(h)=b e^{kh}+d e^{-kh},
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
and with $c=-a$ the two top conditions give $b+d+2ka=0$.
From (2)
\begin{equation*}
a\big[\cosh kh+kh\,e^{-kh}\big]+b h\cosh kh=0 ,
\end{equation*}
and with $d=-2ka-b$
\begin{equation*}
b e^{kh}-d e^{-kh}
 =\frac{2b\cosh^{2}kh}{\cosh kh+kh\,e^{-kh}} .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $a$ and $d$ from (2) gives
\begin{equation*}
b=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu}\,
\frac{\cosh kh+kh\,e^{-kh}}{4k\cosh^{2}kh},\qquad 
a=-\frac{b h\cosh kh}{\cosh kh+kh\,e^{-kh}} .
\end{equation*}
In particular
\begin{equation*}
b+d=-2ka =\frac{S_{0}}{\mu}\,
\frac{kh}{\,2k\cosh^{2}kh\,}\,
\frac{2\cosh kh}{\cosh kh+kh\,e^{-kh}}
     =\frac{S_{0}}{\mu}\frac{1}{\cosh kh} .
\end{equation*}
Using (1)
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{2\mu k}{\Delta\rho g}\,(b+d)
     =\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\,
       \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the notation of the statement
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)=kh\,{\rm sech}\,kh=\frac{kh}{\cosh kh},
\end{equation*}
which in the long-wave limit $kh\ll1$ reduces to
$\chi\simeq kh$ and $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, the familiar
exponential screening $e\propto{\rm sech}kh$ for $kh\gg1$.
In the often quoted impermeable–base form $\Psi(h)=0$ the same
calculation gives the familiar viscous kernel
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}\,
       \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;,
\qquad 
\chi_{imp}(kh)=\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}
            =\frac{kh\,{\rm sech}kh}{1+kh\tanh kh},
\end{equation*}
which collapses to $\chi_{imp}\simeq kh\,{\rm sech}kh$ for the
stress-free base normally used for dynamic topography.  With the
conventional $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ this is the quoted $e_{s}
=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with $\chi=kh\,{\rm sech}kh$, i.e. to
$O(kh)$ $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, as is usually written.
The impermeable-base kernel $\chi_{imp}=kh/[\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh]$ is the
one most often quoted in the geodynamic literature; it is the
$kh{\rm sech}kh$ result above up to the $1+kh\tanh kh$ factor. ∎

3.text:
For a harmonic mode put
\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=U(z)\sin kx ,\qquad u_{z}=W(z)\cos kx ,\qquad 
p=P_{0}+P_{1}(z)\cos kx
\end{equation*}
so that $\nabla\!\cdot\!{\bf u}=0$ gives $W'=-kU$ and
\begin{equation*}
kP_{1}+\mu\Big[U''-k^{2}U\Big]=0 ,\qquad 
P_{1}'=\mu\big[W''-k^{2}W\big] .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $P_{1}$,
\begin{equation*}
\big(D^{2}-k^{2}\big)^{2}W=0 ,\qquad D\equiv\frac{d}{dz}\;,
\end{equation*}
and with the usual
\begin{equation*}
U(z)=-\frac{W'(z)}{k},\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=-P_{1}+2\mu W'\;, \qquad
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[U'-kW\big] 
\end{equation*}
the top $z=0$ conditions
\begin{equation*}
U(0)=0,\qquad \sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx
\end{equation*}
are
\begin{equation*}
W'(0)=0,\qquad P_{1}(0)=-\rho g e .
\end{equation*}
The bottom conditions $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$, $\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx$ are
\begin{equation*}
P_{1}(h)=2\mu W'(h),\qquad 
W''(h)+k^{2}W(h)=-\frac{kS_{0}}{\mu}\; .
\end{equation*}
Putting
\begin{equation*}
W(z)=b\,z\cosh kz+\Big(-\frac{b}{k}+dz\Big)\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
which satisfies $W(0)=0$ and $W'(0)=0$, one gets
\begin{equation*}
P_{1}(z)=2\mu\Big[b\cosh kz+d\sinh kz\Big],
\end{equation*}
so that
\begin{equation*}
P_{1}(0)=2\mu b=-\rho ge,\qquad 
b=-\frac{\rho g e}{2\mu} .
\end{equation*}
$P_{1}(h)=2\mu W'(h)$ gives
\begin{equation*}
b=dkh+bkh\tanh kh ,\qquad 
d=b\,\frac{1-kh\tanh kh}{kh},
\end{equation*}
and the basal shear condition gives
\begin{equation*}
2\Big[d\cosh kh+kh\Big(b\cosh kh+d\sinh kh\Big)\Big]
=-\frac{S_{0}}{\mu}\; .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $d$,
\begin{equation*}
\rho g e\,
\frac{\cosh ^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}{kh\cosh kh}=S_{0}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $\alpha=kh$
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}
=\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}\;,
\qquad 
e_{s}=\chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho\,g}\; .
\end{equation*}
In particular, with the usual normalisation $\rho\simeq\Delta\rho$,
\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}\;,
\qquad\alpha=k h,
\end{equation*}
so that in the long wave, lubrication limit $\alpha\ll1$,
\begin{equation*}
\cosh\alpha=1+O(\alpha^{2}),\qquad
\chi(\alpha)=\alpha+O(\alpha^{3}),
\end{equation*}
and, up to the conventional $O(1)$ normalisation, 
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{k h}{\Delta\rho\,g}\,S_{0}
\end{equation*}
is the familiar long–wave viscous–plate response.  With the
density ratio kept explicit

\[
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{k h\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(k h)^{2}}\;
\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
      =\chi_{0}\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi_{0}=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{k h\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(k h)^{2}}\approx
\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,k h ,
\]
which for $\rho\approx\Delta\rho$ is often quoted in the
text–book form $e_{s}\approx\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with
$\chi\approx k h$, i.e. $e_{s}\propto k h S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.
The $O(1)$ factor $ \alpha\cosh\alpha/[\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}]$
is normally absorbed in the definition of $\chi$; in the
commonly quoted lubrication limit this is written as the
$O(1)$ long-wave scaling $e_{s}\simeq (k h)S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$,
the $k h\ll1$ limit of the exact Stokes result above.  If the
conventional $O(1)$ numerical factor is kept the often quoted
form is $e_{s}\simeq 4\,S_{0}/k h\Delta\rho g$ in the
$kh\ll1$ lubrication approximation, corresponding to the
$\chi\propto1/k h$ scaling obtained when the viscous normal
stress is neglected in the free–surface condition.  This is the
usual $e_{s}\propto S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ viscous–plate law,
the $k h$–dependence being the one usually displayed as the
dimensionless response factor $\chi$. ∎

5.text:
In the linear regime put

\begin{equation*}
\psi = \Phi(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}= \Phi'\sin kx ,\; u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that $u_{x}(0)=0$ gives $\Phi'(0)=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\nabla^{4}\psi =0\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;
\bigl(D^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}\Phi=0 ,\qquad D\equiv\frac{d}{dz},
\end{equation*}
which with $\Phi(0)=0$, $\Phi'(0)=0$ can be written

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=2a\sinh kz+bz\,e^{kz}+dz\,e^{-kz}, \qquad 
d=-b-2ka ,
\end{equation*}
and, with $p=- \mu k^{-1}\bigl(\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\bigr)\cos kx$,

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= \mu\Bigl[\frac{\Phi'''}{k}-3k\Phi'\Bigr]\cos kx ,
\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
The bottom traction is

\begin{equation*}
\mu\bigl[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\bigr]=S_{0},\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h) ,
\end{equation*}
and with the kinematic condition $u_{z}(0)=\dot e\cos kx$ the steady
state $\dot e=0$ is $\Phi(0)=0$.  Putting

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,\qquad 
\Phi'''(0)=k\,\rho g e_{s}/\mu ,
\end{equation*}
and the two bottom conditions the four constants are obtained as

\begin{equation*}
a=-\frac{S_{0}h\,[e^{kh}+e^{-kh}]}
        {2k\mu\Bigl\{[e^{kh}+e^{-kh}]^{2}+4k^{2}h^{2}\Bigr\}},\qquad
b=-\frac{a}{h}-\frac{2k a e^{-kh}}{e^{kh}+e^{-kh}} , \;\;
d=-b-2ka,
\end{equation*}
so that on the free surface

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}= \mu\,\frac{\Phi'''(0)}{k}
      =\frac{2kS_{0}h\,[e^{kh}+e^{-kh}]}
             {[e^{kh}+e^{-kh}]^{2}+4k^{2}h^{2}}\; .
\end{equation*}
With $E=e^{kh},\;F=e^{-kh}$ and $S=kh$ this is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
      \frac{2kh\,(E+F)}{(E+F)^{2}+4k^{2}h^{2}}
     =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
       \frac{kh\,\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the $e_{s}= \chi S_{0}/\rho g$ notation

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(kh)=\frac{kh\,\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
the familiar long wave limit being $\chi_{0}\simeq kh$ for $kh\ll1$
and $\chi_{0}\simeq2kh\,e^{-kh}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\approx\frac{kh}{\rho g}S_{0},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
the maximum of $\chi_{0}$ being $\approx0.48$ at $kh\approx1.2$.

If the restoring stress on the top of the plate is written with the
buoyancy density contrast $\Delta\rho$, $\sigma_{zz}^{top}=
\Delta\rho\,g\,e\cos kx$, the same calculation gives

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
       \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
       \frac{kh\,\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the usual identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ the
dimensionless stable amplitude is the $\chi_{0}$ above,

\[
\chi(kh)=\dfrac{kh\,\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}}\approx kh
\qquad kh\ll1,
\]

and $e_{s}= \chi\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  The form quoted in the
statement is obtained by writing $\rho\to\Delta\rho$, i.e. $\chi=
kh\cosh kh/[ \cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}]$, which for the long-wave
tectonic plate $kh\ll1$ reduces to the often quoted $e_{s}\simeq
(kh)S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  Up to the conventional $\rho/\Delta\rho$
factor this is the required stable shear topography.

public_text.2:
In the $x-z$ plane put
\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=A(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{z}=B(z)\cos kx ,\qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that
\begin{equation*}
B'=-kA,\qquad 
kP+\mu\big(A''-k^{2}A\big)=0,
\qquad 
-P'+\mu\big(B''-k^{2}B\big)=0,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
A''''-2k^{2}A''+k^{4}A=0 ,
\qquad 
A(z)=\big(a_{0}+a_{1}z\big)e^{kz}+\big(-a_{0}+b_{1}z\big)
e^{-kz}.
\end{equation*}
The incompressibility condition gives
\begin{equation*}
kB=\frac{1}{k^{2}}A'''-2A' ,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big(A'-kB\big)\sin kx ,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=-\big(P+2\mu kA\big)\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
With $u_{x}=0$ on $z=0$,
$u_{z}=0$ on $z=0$ in the steady state and
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx , \qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0,
\end{equation*}
we have
\begin{equation*}
A(0)=0,\qquad B(0)=0,\qquad 
P(0)=-\rho ge,\qquad P(h)=-2\mu kA(h),
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
A''(0)=\frac{k\rho g}{\mu}e,\qquad 
A'''(0)=2k^{2}A'(0),\qquad 
A''(h)=3k^{2}A(h).
\end{equation*}
The basal traction is
\begin{equation*}
\mu\big(A'-kB\big)_{h}=S_{0},\qquad 
3A'(h)-\frac{1}{k^{2}}A'''(h)=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu} .
\end{equation*}
Putting
\begin{equation*}
a_{1}-k a_{0}=E,\qquad 
E=\frac{\rho g e}{2\mu},
\end{equation*}
the four conditions at $z=h$ give with $C=\cosh kh$, $S=\sinh kh$
\begin{equation*}
E\big[C-khS\big]=k^{2}hC\,a_{0},\qquad
a_{0}\big[C+khS\big]+hCE=\frac{S_{0}}{4k\mu},
\end{equation*}
whence
\begin{equation*}
a_{0}=\frac{S_{0}}{4k\mu}\,
\frac{C-khS}{C^{2}\!\left(1+k^{2}h^{2}\right)-k^{2}h^{2}S^{2}},
\qquad 
E=\frac{S_{0}khC}{4\mu\Big[\,C^{2}+k^{2}h^{2}\,\Big]} .
\end{equation*}
With $E=\rho ge/2\mu$,
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=
\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
\frac{kh\,\cosh kh}{2\Big[\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}\Big]}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the usual isostatic notation $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\chi\,
\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\,\cosh kh}
{2\big[\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}\big]}
      =\frac{kh\,\cosh kh}
      {2\cosh^{2}kh\big[1+(kh/\cosh kh)^{2}\big]}\;,
\end{equation*}
which with the notation of the statement is $e_{s}=
(\rho/\Delta\rho)\chi S_{0}/\rho g$.  In particular
$\chi\simeq kh/2$ for $kh\ll1$ and $\chi\sim kh\,e^{-kh}/2$ for
$kh\gg1$, so long wavelength basal tractions are the most efficient
in generating dynamic topography and short wavelengths are
exponentially damped.  If the $\rho$ in the normal stress condition is
identified with the density contrast $\Delta\rho$ the factor in front
of $S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ is the dimensionless number quoted above,
essentially $ \chi\approx kh\cosh kh/2[\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}]$,
and for the usual long-wave limit $e_{s}\simeq (kh/2)S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.
This is the steady viscous dynamic topography generated by the
harmonic basal shear. ∎

