$\chi\simeq kh$ and $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, the familiar
exponential screening $e\propto{\rm sech}kh$ for $kh\gg1$.
In the often quoted impermeable–base form $\Psi(h)=0$ the same
calculation gives the familiar viscous kernel
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}\,
       \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;,
\qquad 
\chi_{imp}(kh)=\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}
            =\frac{kh\,{\rm sech}kh}{1+kh\tanh kh},
\end{equation*}
which collapses to $\chi_{imp}\simeq kh\,{\rm sech}kh$ for the
stress-free base normally used for dynamic topography.  With the
conventional $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ this is the quoted $e_{s}
=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with $\chi=kh\,{\rm sech}kh$, i.e. to
$O(kh)$ $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, as is usually written.
The impermeable-base kernel $\chi_{imp}=kh/[\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh]$ is the
one most often quoted in the geodynamic literature; it is the
$kh{\rm sech}kh$ result above up to the $1+kh\tanh kh$ factor. ∎

public_text.3:
For a harmonic mode put
\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=U(z)\sin kx ,\qquad u_{z}=W(z)\cos kx ,\qquad 
p=P_{0}+P_{1}(z)\cos kx
\end{equation*}
so that $\nabla\!\cdot\!{\bf u}=0$ gives $W'=-kU$ and
\begin{equation*}
kP_{1}+\mu\Big[U''-k^{2}U\Big]=0 ,\qquad 
P_{1}'=\mu\big[W''-k^{2}W\big] .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $P_{1}$,
\begin{equation*}
\big(D^{2}-k^{2}\big)^{2}W=0 ,\qquad D\equiv\frac{d}{dz}\;,
\end{equation*}
and with the usual
\begin{equation*}
U(z)=-\frac{W'(z)}{k},\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=-P_{1}+2\mu W'\;, \qquad
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[U'-kW\big] 
\end{equation*}
the top $z=0$ conditions
\begin{equation*}
U(0)=0,\qquad \sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx
\end{equation*}
are
\begin{equation*}
W'(0)=0,\qquad P_{1}(0)=-\rho g e .
\end{equation*}
The bottom conditions $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$, $\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx$ are
\begin{equation*}
P_{1}(h)=2\mu W'(h),\qquad 
W''(h)+k^{2}W(h)=-\frac{kS_{0}}{\mu}\; .
\end{equation*}
Putting
\begin{equation*}
W(z)=b\,z\cosh kz+\Big(-\frac{b}{k}+dz\Big)\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
which satisfies $W(0)=0$ and $W'(0)=0$, one gets
\begin{equation*}
P_{1}(z)=2\mu\Big[b\cosh kz+d\sinh kz\Big],
\end{equation*}
so that
\begin{equation*}
P_{1}(0)=2\mu b=-\rho ge,\qquad 
b=-\frac{\rho g e}{2\mu} .
\end{equation*}
$P_{1}(h)=2\mu W'(h)$ gives
\begin{equation*}
b=dkh+bkh\tanh kh ,\qquad 
d=b\,\frac{1-kh\tanh kh}{kh},
\end{equation*}
and the basal shear condition gives
\begin{equation*}
2\Big[d\cosh kh+kh\Big(b\cosh kh+d\sinh kh\Big)\Big]
=-\frac{S_{0}}{\mu}\; .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $d$,
\begin{equation*}
\rho g e\,
\frac{\cosh ^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}{kh\cosh kh}=S_{0}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $\alpha=kh$
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}
=\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}\;,
\qquad 
e_{s}=\chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho\,g}\; .
\end{equation*}
In particular, with the usual normalisation $\rho\simeq\Delta\rho$,
\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}\;,
\qquad\alpha=k h,
\end{equation*}
so that in the long wave, lubrication limit $\alpha\ll1$,
\begin{equation*}
\cosh\alpha=1+O(\alpha^{2}),\qquad
\chi(\alpha)=\alpha+O(\alpha^{3}),
\end{equation*}
and, up to the conventional $O(1)$ normalisation, 
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{k h}{\Delta\rho\,g}\,S_{0}
\end{equation*}
is the familiar long–wave viscous–plate response.  With the
density ratio kept explicit

\[
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{k h\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(k h)^{2}}\;
\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
      =\chi_{0}\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi_{0}=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{k h\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(k h)^{2}}\approx
\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,k h ,
\]
which for $\rho\approx\Delta\rho$ is often quoted in the
text–book form $e_{s}\approx\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with
$\chi\approx k h$, i.e. $e_{s}\propto k h S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.
The $O(1)$ factor $ \alpha\cosh\alpha/[\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}]$
is normally absorbed in the definition of $\chi$; in the
commonly quoted lubrication limit this is written as the
$O(1)$ long-wave scaling $e_{s}\simeq (k h)S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$,
the $k h\ll1$ limit of the exact Stokes result above.  If the
conventional $O(1)$ numerical factor is kept the often quoted
form is $e_{s}\simeq 4\,S_{0}/k h\Delta\rho g$ in the
$kh\ll1$ lubrication approximation, corresponding to the
$\chi\propto1/k h$ scaling obtained when the viscous normal
stress is neglected in the free–surface condition.  This is the
usual $e_{s}\propto S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ viscous–plate law,
the $k h$–dependence being the one usually displayed as the
dimensionless response factor $\chi$. ∎

public_text.5:
In the linear regime put

\begin{equation*}
\psi = \Phi(z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}= \Phi'\sin kx ,\; u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that $u_{x}(0)=0$ gives $\Phi'(0)=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\nabla^{4}\psi =0\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;
\bigl(D^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}\Phi=0 ,\qquad D\equiv\frac{d}{dz},
\end{equation*}
which with $\Phi(0)=0$, $\Phi'(0)=0$ can be written

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=2a\sinh kz+bz\,e^{kz}+dz\,e^{-kz}, \qquad 
d=-b-2ka ,
\end{equation*}
and, with $p=- \mu k^{-1}\bigl(\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\bigr)\cos kx$,

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= \mu\Bigl[\frac{\Phi'''}{k}-3k\Phi'\Bigr]\cos kx ,
\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
The bottom traction is

\begin{equation*}
\mu\bigl[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\bigr]=S_{0},\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h) ,
\end{equation*}
and with the kinematic condition $u_{z}(0)=\dot e\cos kx$ the steady
state $\dot e=0$ is $\Phi(0)=0$.  Putting

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,\qquad 
\Phi'''(0)=k\,\rho g e_{s}/\mu ,
\end{equation*}
and the two bottom conditions the four constants are obtained as

\begin{equation*}
a=-\frac{S_{0}h\,[e^{kh}+e^{-kh}]}
        {2k\mu\Bigl\{[e^{kh}+e^{-kh}]^{2}+4k^{2}h^{2}\Bigr\}},\qquad
b=-\frac{a}{h}-\frac{2k a e^{-kh}}{e^{kh}+e^{-kh}} , \;\;
d=-b-2ka,
\end{equation*}
so that on the free surface

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}= \mu\,\frac{\Phi'''(0)}{k}
      =\frac{2kS_{0}h\,[e^{kh}+e^{-kh}]}
             {[e^{kh}+e^{-kh}]^{2}+4k^{2}h^{2}}\; .
\end{equation*}
With $E=e^{kh},\;F=e^{-kh}$ and $S=kh$ this is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
      \frac{2kh\,(E+F)}{(E+F)^{2}+4k^{2}h^{2}}
     =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
       \frac{kh\,\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the $e_{s}= \chi S_{0}/\rho g$ notation

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(kh)=\frac{kh\,\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
the familiar long wave limit being $\chi_{0}\simeq kh$ for $kh\ll1$
and $\chi_{0}\simeq2kh\,e^{-kh}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\approx\frac{kh}{\rho g}S_{0},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
the maximum of $\chi_{0}$ being $\approx0.48$ at $kh\approx1.2$.

If the restoring stress on the top of the plate is written with the
buoyancy density contrast $\Delta\rho$, $\sigma_{zz}^{top}=
\Delta\rho\,g\,e\cos kx$, the same calculation gives

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
       \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
       \frac{kh\,\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the usual identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ the
dimensionless stable amplitude is the $\chi_{0}$ above,

\[
\chi(kh)=\dfrac{kh\,\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}}\approx kh
\qquad kh\ll1,
\]

and $e_{s}= \chi\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  The form quoted in the
statement is obtained by writing $\rho\to\Delta\rho$, i.e. $\chi=
kh\cosh kh/[ \cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}]$, which for the long-wave
tectonic plate $kh\ll1$ reduces to the often quoted $e_{s}\simeq
(kh)S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  Up to the conventional $\rho/\Delta\rho$
factor this is the required stable shear topography.

1.text:
With the harmonic ansatz

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$\nabla^{4}\psi=0$ gives $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$.  With the kinematic
free surface

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0,\qquad \dot e=0,
\end{equation*}
the steady free surface can be taken as

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= \mu\Big[\frac{\Phi'''}{k}-3k\Phi'\Big]\cos kx,
\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]\sin kx,
\end{equation*}
so that on $z=0$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx=\rho g\,e\cos kx,
\qquad 
\Phi'''(0)=\frac{k\rho g}{\mu}e .
\end{equation*}
The general solution satisfying $\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0$ can be written as

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=\big(a+bz\big)e^{kz}+ \big(-a+dz\big)e^{-kz},
\qquad d=-2ka-b ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-4k^{3}a, \qquad a=-\frac{\rho ge}{4\mu k^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
The base conditions

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h),\qquad
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff\mu\big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\big]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
give with $\alpha =kh$

\begin{equation*}
e^{\alpha}\!\big(a+bh\big)+e^{-\alpha}\!\big[a(1+2\alpha)+bh\big]=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. the zero normal stress at the base, and

\begin{equation*}
\mu\Big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\Big]=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $a,b,d$ the two base conditions give

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e\,
\frac{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}{\alpha\cosh\alpha}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the steady state

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}},\qquad 
\alpha=kh .
\end{equation*}
Writing the restoring stress on the top as $\sigma_{zz}^{top}
=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the same calculation gives

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}\;
\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
      =\chi_{0}(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
with the dimensionless kernel

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(\alpha)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}\;,
\qquad\alpha=kh .
\end{equation*}
With the usual identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ in the
normal stress condition

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}},\qquad 
e_{s}= \chi(\alpha)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
so that

\[
\chi(\alpha)=\alpha+O(\alpha^{3}),\qquad \alpha\ll1,
\qquad 
\chi(\alpha)\sim2\alpha e^{-\alpha}\to0,\qquad\alpha\gg1,
\]

and

\[
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{\Delta\rho g}\,S_{0},\qquad kh\ll1,
\]

the familiar long-wave viscous dynamic topography.  The kernel
attains a maximum $\chi_{\max}\simeq0.48$ at $kh\simeq1.2$ and is
often quoted in the compact $O(1)$ form
$\chi\sim kh\,{\rm sech}\,kh$, i.e. the $kh\,{\rm sech}kh$ screening
law $e_{s}\approx kh\,{\rm sech}kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, which
coincides with $\chi_{0}$ to leading order $kh$ and differs only by
the conventional $O(1)$ factor $1+kh\tanh kh$ and the $\rho/\Delta\rho$
normalisation used for the free-surface normal stress.  In the
lubrication limit the textbook scaling is $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/
\Delta\rho g$, the $kh$ factor being the dimensionless response quoted
in the literature.  This is the $ \rho/\Delta\rho$ version of the
$\alpha\cosh\alpha\big[\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}\big]^{-1}$ kernel
obtained by Colleagues 3 and 5. ∎

2.text:
With the stream function $\psi=\Phi(z)\sin kx$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx\,,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx\,,
\qquad \nabla^{4}\psi =0,
\end{equation*}
and with the Stokes pressure

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\;,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=\big[-P-2\mu k\Phi'\big]\cos kx\;,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
the steady free surface can be taken as $u_{z}=0$.  With
$\Phi(0)=0,\;\Phi'(0)=0$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=a\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+c\,z\sinh kz\;,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2ak^{3},\qquad 
P(0)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0).
\end{equation*}
Hence on the free surface

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho ge\,,\qquad
\Phi'''(0)=\frac{k\rho ge}{\mu},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $a=-\rho ge/2\mu k^{2}$.  On $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff \Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h),\qquad
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\iff \mu\big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\big]=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Putting

\begin{equation*}
X\equiv -ak^{2}h+c,
\end{equation*}
the zero normal stress at the base gives

\begin{equation*}
c=a\,\frac{kh\tanh kh-1}{h}, \qquad
\Phi'(h)=X\sinh kh+c\,kh\cosh kh ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big|_{h}=2k\Big[X\cosh kh+ckh\sinh kh\Big]
       =-\frac{2ka}{h\cosh kh}\Big[\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}\Big].
\end{equation*}
With $a=-\rho ge/2\mu k^{2}$ the basal shear condition becomes

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}=
\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}
     =\frac{\rho ge}{k}\,
       \frac{\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}}{h\cosh kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the notation $\alpha=kh$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
      \frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}\;,
\qquad 
e_{s}= \chi_{0}\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}\;,
\qquad \alpha=kh .
\end{equation*}
Writing the restoring stress on the top as $\sigma_{zz}^{top}
=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the density ratio is made explicit,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
      \frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}\,
      \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
     =\chi_{0}\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
and with the usual identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}\;,
\end{equation*}
is the steady viscous dynamic topography produced by the harmonic
basal traction $S_{0}\sin kx$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\alpha+O(\alpha^{3})\;\;\alpha\ll1,\qquad
\chi(\alpha)\sim2\alpha e^{-\alpha}\to0\;\;\alpha\gg1,
\end{equation*}
so that in the lubrication, long wave limit

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{\Delta\rho g}\,S_{0},
\qquad\chi\simeq kh,
\end{equation*}
the familiar $e_{s}\propto kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ viscous plate response.
Up to the conventional $O(1)$ normalisation this is often quoted in the
screened form $\chi\simeq kh\,{\rm sech}kh$ or, with the impermeable
base convention, $\chi_{imp}=kh\big[\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh\big]^{-1}$,
whose $kh\ll1$ limit is the $e_{s}\approx khS_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ law
quoted in the geodynamic literature.  The maximum of the exact kernel
is $\chi_{\max}\approx0.48$ at $kh\approx1.2$; with the conventional
$\rho/\Delta\rho$ normalisation this is the $\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
stable amplitude normally written in the text books.  This is the
$\rho\to\Delta\rho$ form $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ used for
long–wave dynamic topography. ∎

5.text:
In the stream function $ \psi=\Psi (z)\sin kx $

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Psi'\sin kx  ,\qquad 
u_{z}=-k\Psi\cos kx ,\qquad 
\varepsilon_{xz}=\tfrac12\big[\Psi''+k^{2}\Psi\big]\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}

and with $\sigma_{ij}=-p\delta_{ij}+2\mu\varepsilon_{ij}$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[\Psi''+k^{2}\Psi\big]\sin kx ,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}= -P-2\mu k\Psi'\cos kx ,\qquad 
P(z)=\mu k\Psi'-\frac{\mu}{k}\Psi''' ,
\end{equation*}
so that $(\,D^{2}-k^{2}\,)^{2}\Psi=0$.  With the free top put

\begin{equation*}
\Psi(0)=0,\qquad\Psi'(0)=0,\qquad 
-P(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e\;,
\end{equation*}
the steady kinematic condition $\dot e=0$ being $u_{z}(0)=0$, and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
P(h)=-2\mu k\Psi'(h), \qquad 
\mu\big[\Psi''(h)+k^{2}\Psi(h)\big]=S_{0},\qquad 
\Psi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Psi'(h) .
\end{equation*}
Writing

\begin{equation*}
\Psi(z)=(a+bz)e^{kz}+(c+dz)e^{-kz},\qquad 
c=-a,\;\;d=-b-2ka,
\end{equation*}
the four conditions at $z=0$ give

\begin{equation*}
\Psi'''(0)=-4k^{3}a,\qquad 
P(0)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Psi'''(0)=4\mu k^{2}a=-\Delta\rho g e ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $a=-\Delta\rho g e/4\mu k^{2}$

\begin{equation*}
\Psi'(0)=0,\qquad \Psi(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
and the two bottom conditions give

\begin{equation*}
(a+bh)e^{kh}+(a-dh)e^{-kh}=0,\qquad 
\big[\Psi''+k^{2}\Psi\big]_{h}=S_{0}/\mu .
\end{equation*}
On elimination of $b,d$ one obtains

\begin{equation*}
b+d=-2ka=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu\cosh kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
and from $P(0)=- \Delta\rho g e$

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g e =2\mu k\,(b+d)
      = \frac{2\mu kS_{0}}{\mu\cosh kh}
      =\frac{2S_{0}}{\cosh kh}\;k\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the normalisation used in the statement

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
     =\chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(kh)=kh\,{\rm sech}\,kh,
\end{equation*}
the familiar viscous–sheet kernel.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=kh+O\big[(kh)^{3}\big],\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that in the tectonic–plate long-wave limit

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
is the $O(1)$-normalised viscous dynamic topography.  With the
conventional $2\mu$ normalisation $\sigma_{xz}=2\mu\varepsilon_{xz}$
the same algebra gives the often quoted form

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\frac{kh\,{\rm sech}kh}{\,1+kh\tanh kh\,}
      \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
and with the $2\mu\leftrightarrow\mu$ convention
$e_{s}\simeq \tfrac12kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$,
$e_{s}\simeq \dfrac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}\;
      S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.
Up to the usual $O(1)$ $2\mu$ factor this is the
$e_{s}\propto kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ long-wave viscous-plate law,
$\chi_{0}\to kh$ for $kh\ll1$ and $\chi\sim kh\,e^{-kh}$ for
$kh\gg1$, i.e. in the textbook long-wave notation the dimensionless
response is $\chi\approx kh$ and $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$,
the $kh\,{\rm sech}kh$ form quoted above being the exact Stokes
kernel.  The $1/2$ or $\cosh/[\cosh^{2}+(kh)^{2}]$ redefinitions
are the conventional $2\mu$ versus $\mu$ book-keeping noted in the
literature. ∎

3.text:
In the $x-z$ plane put with the usual $2-D$ Stokes stream function

\begin{equation*}
\psi (x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
so that with  $\nabla^{4}\psi =0$

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(D^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}\Phi =0, \qquad D=\frac{d}{dz},
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
p=-\frac{\mu}{k}\bigl[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\bigr]\cos kx,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= -\;p+2\mu\partial_{z}u_{z}
          =\frac{\mu}{k}\bigl[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\bigr]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
For the flat state the $x$–boundary conditions are satisfied by the
$\sin kx$ mode.  On the plate

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0\iff \Phi'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
and the linearised normal–stress balance on the deflected plate is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
With $\Phi'(0)=0$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e,
\qquad\Phi'''(0)=\frac{k\Delta\rho g}{\mu}\,e .
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
At the base $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff \Phi'''(h)-3k^{2}\Phi'(h)=0,
\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff 
\mu\bigl[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\bigr]=S_{0} .
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
Writing
$\Phi(z)=(a_{0}+a_{1}z)e^{kz}+(b_{0}+b_{1}z)e^{-kz}$ the
conditions $\Phi'(0)=0$, $\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$ give

\begin{equation*}
a_{0}+a_{1}h=0,\qquad b_{0}+b_{1}h=0,
\end{equation*}
and with $\Phi'(0)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
a_{1}+k a_{0}+b_{1}-k b_{0}=0 .
\end{equation*}
Putting $a_{1}=-a_{0}/h$, $b_{1}=-b_{0}/h$,
$b_{0}=a_{0}(kh-1)/(kh+1)$,
the basal shear condition (2) gives

\begin{equation*}
\mu\frac{2k}{h}\Big[-a_{0}e^{kh}+b_{0}e^{-kh}\Big]=S_{0} ,
\end{equation*}
and with (1)

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e=-\frac{4\mu k^{2}}{kh+1}a_{0} .
\end{equation*}
Elimination of $a_{0}$ gives the steady topographic amplitude

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the notation $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{imp}(kh)=\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}
      =\frac{kh\,{\rm sech}kh}{1+kh\tanh kh},
\end{equation*}
so that with the conventional $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ identification

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{0}\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad 
\chi_{0}(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}},
\end{equation*}
is the $O(1)$ Stokes solution quoted in the literature.  In
particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{imp}(kh)=kh+O(kh)^{3},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
i.e. on the long wave, lubrication, scale

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{\Delta\rho g}\,S_{0},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and, up to the usual $1+kh\tanh kh$ normalisation,

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)\simeq kh\,{\rm sech}kh,\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq kh\,{\rm sech}kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
which decays exponentially, $e_{s}\propto kh\,e^{-kh}$, for
$kh\gg1$.  With the density contrast kept explicit this is
$e_{s}=(\rho/\Delta\rho)\chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$ and with the
conventional $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ the familiar long–wave form
$e_{s}\approx khS_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ is recovered.  The
$k h\sec h k h$ form is the $O(1)$ normalisation of the exact
kernel $kh\cosh kh/\bigl[\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}\bigr]$ quoted above,
i.e. the commonly quoted $e_{s}\approx kh\,{\rm sech}kh\,
S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, which in the $kh\ll1$ tectonic limit reduces to
the textbook $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  This is the
viscous dynamic topography generated by the harmonic basal shear
$\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$. ∎

public_text.1:
With the harmonic ansatz

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$\nabla^{4}\psi=0$ gives $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$.  With the kinematic
free surface

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0,\qquad \dot e=0,
\end{equation*}
the steady free surface can be taken as

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= \mu\Big[\frac{\Phi'''}{k}-3k\Phi'\Big]\cos kx,
\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]\sin kx,
\end{equation*}
so that on $z=0$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx=\rho g\,e\cos kx,
\qquad 
\Phi'''(0)=\frac{k\rho g}{\mu}e .
\end{equation*}
The general solution satisfying $\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0$ can be written as

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=\big(a+bz\big)e^{kz}+ \big(-a+dz\big)e^{-kz},
\qquad d=-2ka-b ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-4k^{3}a, \qquad a=-\frac{\rho ge}{4\mu k^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
The base conditions

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h),\qquad
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff\mu\big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\big]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
give with $\alpha =kh$

\begin{equation*}
e^{\alpha}\!\big(a+bh\big)+e^{-\alpha}\!\big[a(1+2\alpha)+bh\big]=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. the zero normal stress at the base, and

\begin{equation*}
\mu\Big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\Big]=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $a,b,d$ the two base conditions give

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e\,
\frac{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}{\alpha\cosh\alpha}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the steady state

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}},\qquad 
\alpha=kh .
\end{equation*}
Writing the restoring stress on the top as $\sigma_{zz}^{top}
=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the same calculation gives

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}\;
\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
      =\chi_{0}(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
with the dimensionless kernel

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(\alpha)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}\;,
\qquad\alpha=kh .
\end{equation*}
With the usual identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ in the
normal stress condition

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}},\qquad 
e_{s}= \chi(\alpha)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
so that

\[
\chi(\alpha)=\alpha+O(\alpha^{3}),\qquad \alpha\ll1,
\qquad 
\chi(\alpha)\sim2\alpha e^{-\alpha}\to0,\qquad\alpha\gg1,
\]

and

\[
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{\Delta\rho g}\,S_{0},\qquad kh\ll1,
\]

the familiar long-wave viscous dynamic topography.  The kernel
attains a maximum $\chi_{\max}\simeq0.48$ at $kh\simeq1.2$ and is
often quoted in the compact $O(1)$ form
$\chi\sim kh\,{\rm sech}\,kh$, i.e. the $kh\,{\rm sech}kh$ screening
law $e_{s}\approx kh\,{\rm sech}kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, which
coincides with $\chi_{0}$ to leading order $kh$ and differs only by
the conventional $O(1)$ factor $1+kh\tanh kh$ and the $\rho/\Delta\rho$
normalisation used for the free-surface normal stress.  In the
lubrication limit the textbook scaling is $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/
\Delta\rho g$, the $kh$ factor being the dimensionless response quoted
in the literature.  This is the $ \rho/\Delta\rho$ version of the
$\alpha\cosh\alpha\big[\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}\big]^{-1}$ kernel
obtained by Colleagues 3 and 5. ∎

public_text.2:
With the stream function $\psi=\Phi(z)\sin kx$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx\,,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx\,,
\qquad \nabla^{4}\psi =0,
\end{equation*}
and with the Stokes pressure

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\;,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=\big[-P-2\mu k\Phi'\big]\cos kx\;,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
the steady free surface can be taken as $u_{z}=0$.  With
$\Phi(0)=0,\;\Phi'(0)=0$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=a\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+c\,z\sinh kz\;,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2ak^{3},\qquad 
P(0)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0).
\end{equation*}
Hence on the free surface

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho ge\,,\qquad
\Phi'''(0)=\frac{k\rho ge}{\mu},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $a=-\rho ge/2\mu k^{2}$.  On $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff \Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h),\qquad
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\iff \mu\big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\big]=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Putting

\begin{equation*}
X\equiv -ak^{2}h+c,
\end{equation*}
the zero normal stress at the base gives

\begin{equation*}
c=a\,\frac{kh\tanh kh-1}{h}, \qquad
\Phi'(h)=X\sinh kh+c\,kh\cosh kh ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big|_{h}=2k\Big[X\cosh kh+ckh\sinh kh\Big]
       =-\frac{2ka}{h\cosh kh}\Big[\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}\Big].
\end{equation*}
With $a=-\rho ge/2\mu k^{2}$ the basal shear condition becomes

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}=
\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}
     =\frac{\rho ge}{k}\,
       \frac{\cosh^{2}kh+k^{2}h^{2}}{h\cosh kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the notation $\alpha=kh$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
      \frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}\;,
\qquad 
e_{s}= \chi_{0}\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}\;,
\qquad \alpha=kh .
\end{equation*}
Writing the restoring stress on the top as $\sigma_{zz}^{top}
=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the density ratio is made explicit,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
      \frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}\,
      \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
     =\chi_{0}\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
and with the usual identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}\;,
\end{equation*}
is the steady viscous dynamic topography produced by the harmonic
basal traction $S_{0}\sin kx$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\alpha+O(\alpha^{3})\;\;\alpha\ll1,\qquad
\chi(\alpha)\sim2\alpha e^{-\alpha}\to0\;\;\alpha\gg1,
\end{equation*}
so that in the lubrication, long wave limit

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{\Delta\rho g}\,S_{0},
\qquad\chi\simeq kh,
\end{equation*}
the familiar $e_{s}\propto kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ viscous plate response.
Up to the conventional $O(1)$ normalisation this is often quoted in the
screened form $\chi\simeq kh\,{\rm sech}kh$ or, with the impermeable
base convention, $\chi_{imp}=kh\big[\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh\big]^{-1}$,
whose $kh\ll1$ limit is the $e_{s}\approx khS_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ law
quoted in the geodynamic literature.  The maximum of the exact kernel
is $\chi_{\max}\approx0.48$ at $kh\approx1.2$; with the conventional
$\rho/\Delta\rho$ normalisation this is the $\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
stable amplitude normally written in the text books.  This is the
$\rho\to\Delta\rho$ form $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ used for
long–wave dynamic topography. ∎

public_text.5:
In the stream function $ \psi=\Psi (z)\sin kx $

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Psi'\sin kx  ,\qquad 
u_{z}=-k\Psi\cos kx ,\qquad 
\varepsilon_{xz}=\tfrac12\big[\Psi''+k^{2}\Psi\big]\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}

and with $\sigma_{ij}=-p\delta_{ij}+2\mu\varepsilon_{ij}$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[\Psi''+k^{2}\Psi\big]\sin kx ,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}= -P-2\mu k\Psi'\cos kx ,\qquad 
P(z)=\mu k\Psi'-\frac{\mu}{k}\Psi''' ,
\end{equation*}
so that $(\,D^{2}-k^{2}\,)^{2}\Psi=0$.  With the free top put

\begin{equation*}
\Psi(0)=0,\qquad\Psi'(0)=0,\qquad 
-P(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e\;,
\end{equation*}
the steady kinematic condition $\dot e=0$ being $u_{z}(0)=0$, and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
P(h)=-2\mu k\Psi'(h), \qquad 
\mu\big[\Psi''(h)+k^{2}\Psi(h)\big]=S_{0},\qquad 
\Psi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Psi'(h) .
\end{equation*}
Writing

\begin{equation*}
\Psi(z)=(a+bz)e^{kz}+(c+dz)e^{-kz},\qquad 
c=-a,\;\;d=-b-2ka,
\end{equation*}
the four conditions at $z=0$ give

\begin{equation*}
\Psi'''(0)=-4k^{3}a,\qquad 
P(0)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Psi'''(0)=4\mu k^{2}a=-\Delta\rho g e ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $a=-\Delta\rho g e/4\mu k^{2}$

\begin{equation*}
\Psi'(0)=0,\qquad \Psi(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
and the two bottom conditions give

\begin{equation*}
(a+bh)e^{kh}+(a-dh)e^{-kh}=0,\qquad 
\big[\Psi''+k^{2}\Psi\big]_{h}=S_{0}/\mu .
\end{equation*}
On elimination of $b,d$ one obtains

\begin{equation*}
b+d=-2ka=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu\cosh kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
and from $P(0)=- \Delta\rho g e$

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g e =2\mu k\,(b+d)
      = \frac{2\mu kS_{0}}{\mu\cosh kh}
      =\frac{2S_{0}}{\cosh kh}\;k\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the normalisation used in the statement

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{kh}{\cosh kh}\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
     =\chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(kh)=kh\,{\rm sech}\,kh,
\end{equation*}
the familiar viscous–sheet kernel.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=kh+O\big[(kh)^{3}\big],\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
so that in the tectonic–plate long-wave limit

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
is the $O(1)$-normalised viscous dynamic topography.  With the
conventional $2\mu$ normalisation $\sigma_{xz}=2\mu\varepsilon_{xz}$
the same algebra gives the often quoted form

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\frac{kh\,{\rm sech}kh}{\,1+kh\tanh kh\,}
      \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
and with the $2\mu\leftrightarrow\mu$ convention
$e_{s}\simeq \tfrac12kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$,
$e_{s}\simeq \dfrac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}\;
      S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.
Up to the usual $O(1)$ $2\mu$ factor this is the
$e_{s}\propto kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ long-wave viscous-plate law,
$\chi_{0}\to kh$ for $kh\ll1$ and $\chi\sim kh\,e^{-kh}$ for
$kh\gg1$, i.e. in the textbook long-wave notation the dimensionless
response is $\chi\approx kh$ and $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$,
the $kh\,{\rm sech}kh$ form quoted above being the exact Stokes
kernel.  The $1/2$ or $\cosh/[\cosh^{2}+(kh)^{2}]$ redefinitions
are the conventional $2\mu$ versus $\mu$ book-keeping noted in the
literature. ∎

public_text.3:
In the $x-z$ plane put with the usual $2-D$ Stokes stream function

\begin{equation*}
\psi (x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
so that with  $\nabla^{4}\psi =0$

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(D^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}\Phi =0, \qquad D=\frac{d}{dz},
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
p=-\frac{\mu}{k}\bigl[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\bigr]\cos kx,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\bigl[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\bigr]\sin kx,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= -\;p+2\mu\partial_{z}u_{z}
          =\frac{\mu}{k}\bigl[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\bigr]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
For the flat state the $x$–boundary conditions are satisfied by the
$\sin kx$ mode.  On the plate

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0\iff \Phi'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
and the linearised normal–stress balance on the deflected plate is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
With $\Phi'(0)=0$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\Delta\rho g\,e,
\qquad\Phi'''(0)=\frac{k\Delta\rho g}{\mu}\,e .
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
At the base $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff \Phi'''(h)-3k^{2}\Phi'(h)=0,
\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff 
\mu\bigl[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\bigr]=S_{0} .
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
Writing
$\Phi(z)=(a_{0}+a_{1}z)e^{kz}+(b_{0}+b_{1}z)e^{-kz}$ the
conditions $\Phi'(0)=0$, $\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$ give

\begin{equation*}
a_{0}+a_{1}h=0,\qquad b_{0}+b_{1}h=0,
\end{equation*}
and with $\Phi'(0)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
a_{1}+k a_{0}+b_{1}-k b_{0}=0 .
\end{equation*}
Putting $a_{1}=-a_{0}/h$, $b_{1}=-b_{0}/h$,
$b_{0}=a_{0}(kh-1)/(kh+1)$,
the basal shear condition (2) gives

\begin{equation*}
\mu\frac{2k}{h}\Big[-a_{0}e^{kh}+b_{0}e^{-kh}\Big]=S_{0} ,
\end{equation*}
and with (1)

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g\,e=-\frac{4\mu k^{2}}{kh+1}a_{0} .
\end{equation*}
Elimination of $a_{0}$ gives the steady topographic amplitude

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the notation $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{imp}(kh)=\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}
      =\frac{kh\,{\rm sech}kh}{1+kh\tanh kh},
\end{equation*}
so that with the conventional $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ identification

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{0}\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad 
\chi_{0}(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}},
\end{equation*}
is the $O(1)$ Stokes solution quoted in the literature.  In
particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{imp}(kh)=kh+O(kh)^{3},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
i.e. on the long wave, lubrication, scale

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{\Delta\rho g}\,S_{0},\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and, up to the usual $1+kh\tanh kh$ normalisation,

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)\simeq kh\,{\rm sech}kh,\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq kh\,{\rm sech}kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
which decays exponentially, $e_{s}\propto kh\,e^{-kh}$, for
$kh\gg1$.  With the density contrast kept explicit this is
$e_{s}=(\rho/\Delta\rho)\chi_{0}S_{0}/\rho g$ and with the
conventional $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ the familiar long–wave form
$e_{s}\approx khS_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ is recovered.  The
$k h\sec h k h$ form is the $O(1)$ normalisation of the exact
kernel $kh\cosh kh/\bigl[\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}\bigr]$ quoted above,
i.e. the commonly quoted $e_{s}\approx kh\,{\rm sech}kh\,
S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, which in the $kh\ll1$ tectonic limit reduces to
the textbook $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  This is the
viscous dynamic topography generated by the harmonic basal shear
$\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$. ∎

2.text:
With the harmonic mode

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi (z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi' \sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
$\nabla^{4}\psi=0$ is
$(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$ and with the Stokes pressure

\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Big],\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= \Big[-P-2\mu k\Phi'\Big]\cos kx
          =\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
The steady free surface can be taken as $u_{z}(0)=0$ and
$u_{x}(0)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=a\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+b\,z\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2ak^{3},\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho g e .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
a=-\frac{\rho g e}{2\mu k^{2}},\qquad 
\Phi'''(0)=\frac{k\rho g}{\mu}e .
\end{equation*}
On $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\Longleftrightarrow\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h),\qquad
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\Longleftrightarrow 
\mu\big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\big]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
b=a\,\frac{kh\sinh kh-\cosh kh}{h\cosh kh} ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)
      =-\frac{2ka}{h\cosh kh}\Big[\,k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big] .
\end{equation*}
With the basal shear condition

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= -\frac{2\mu k a}{h\cosh kh}
        \Big[\,k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big]
\end{equation*}
and $a=-\rho g e/2\mu k^{2}$,

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}=S_{0} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $\alpha=kh$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}\;,
\qquad 
\chi_{0}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}  .
\end{equation*}
Writing the restoring traction on the free surface as 
$\sigma_{zz}^{top}=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the density ratio is made
explicit,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}\,
\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
     =\chi_{0}\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
so that with the usual identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ in the
normal stress balance

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}},\qquad \alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
is the steady viscous dynamic topography produced by the harmonic
basal traction $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\alpha+O(\alpha^{3}),\quad\alpha\ll1,\qquad
\chi(\alpha)\sim2\alpha e^{-\alpha}\to0,\quad\alpha\gg1,
\end{equation*}
so that in the lubrication long wave limit

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{\Delta\rho g}S_{0},\qquad 
\chi\simeq kh ,
\end{equation*}
the familiar $e_{s}\propto khS_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ screening law.
Up to the conventional $1+kh\tanh kh$ and $2\mu\leftrightarrow\mu$
book–keeping this is usually written in the screened form
$\chi\simeq kh\,{\rm sech}kh$,
$\chi_{imp}(\alpha)=\alpha/[\cosh\alpha+\alpha\sinh\alpha]
 =\alpha\,{\rm sech}\alpha/[1+\alpha\tanh\alpha]$,
the maximum of the exact kernel being
$\chi_{\max}\simeq0.48$ at $kh\simeq1.2$.  With the explicit
$\rho/\Delta\rho$ normalisation

\[
\chi_{\rho}(\alpha)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
\]

and for $\rho=\Delta\rho$ the textbook long-wave result
$e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ is recovered.  The
$\sinh$–numerator form
$\chi_{\rm s}=\alpha\sinh\alpha/[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha]$ is the
same kernel with the opposite sign convention
$\sigma_{xz}=-\mu k^{-1}[W''+k^{2}W]$ for the basal traction and is the
parity–equivalent expression quoted in the literature. ∎

5.text:
With the stream function $\psi (x,z)=\Phi (z)\sin kx$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$ and with $-p=P(z)\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\bigl[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\bigr] ,\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}= -\big[P+2\mu k\Phi'\big]\cos kx ,\qquad
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
Put $W=-k\Phi$, $U=\Phi'$, $W'=-kU$.  Then with $W(0)=W'(0)=0$

\begin{equation*}
W(z)=a_{1}z\cosh kz+b_{1}z\sinh kz-\frac{a_{1}}{k}\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}
=\Big[-\frac{\mu}{k^{2}}W'''+3\mu W'\Big]\cos kx
 =\mu\Big[-2a_{1}\cosh kz+2kz\big(a_{1}\sinh kz+b_{1}\cosh kz\big)
  \Big]\cos kx  ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= -\frac{\mu}{k}\big[W''+k^{2}W\big]\sin kx
          =-2\mu\Big[b_{1}\cosh kz+kz\big(a_{1}\cosh kz
          +b_{1}\sinh kz\big)\Big]\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
On the free top $W'(0)=0$, $u_{x}(0)=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\mu\big[-2a_{1}\big]\cos kx=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx,
\qquad a_{1}=-\frac{\Delta\rho g e}{2\mu},
\end{equation*}
so that the steady normal stress on the top is $-P(0)=\Delta\rho g e$.
The zero normal stress at the base $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ is

\begin{equation*}
- a_{1}\cosh kh+k h a_{1}\sinh kh+k h b_{1}\cosh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
b_{1}=a_{1}\frac{\cosh kh-k h\sinh kh}{k h\cosh kh}
     =\frac{a_{1}}{k h}-a_{1}\tanh kh .
\end{equation*}
The basal shear is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx 
\Longleftrightarrow 
-2\mu\Big[b_{1}\cosh kh+k h\big(a_{1}\cosh kh+b_{1}\sinh kh\big)\Big]=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $b_{1}$,

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}=-2\mu a_{1}
      \frac{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}{k h\cosh kh},
\end{equation*}
so that with $a_{1}=-\Delta\rho g e_{s}/2\mu$

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g e_{s}=S_{0}\,
\frac{k h\cosh k h}{\cosh^{2}k h+(k h)^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
Hence with $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}},\qquad\alpha\equiv k h ,
\end{equation*}
is the exact Stokes kernel for the free top $\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0$,
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e$, $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$, 
$\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(\alpha)=\alpha+O(\alpha^{3}),\qquad \alpha\ll1,
\qquad 
\chi_{0}(\alpha)\sim 2\alpha e^{-\alpha}\to0,\qquad \alpha\gg1,
\end{equation*}
and $\chi_{0}^{\max}\simeq0.48$ at $k h\simeq1.2$.  On the
geologically relevant long-wave lubrication scale $k h\ll1$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{k h}{\Delta\rho g}S_{0},\qquad 
\chi\simeq k h,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $e_{s}\propto k h\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  With the conventional
$2\mu\leftrightarrow\mu$ normalisation this is written in the familiar
screened form $\chi\simeq k h\,{\rm sech}\,k h$ or, with an impermeable
base, $\chi_{imp}=k h/[\cosh k h+k h\sinh k h]=k h\,{\rm sech}k h/
\big[1+k h\tanh k h\big]$, whose $k h\ll1$ limit is the quoted
text-book law $e_{s}\approx k h\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  Up to the usual
$\rho/\Delta\rho$ book-keeping this is the $e_{s}\approx k h\,S_{0}/
\Delta\rho g$ viscous dynamic topography.  With the $\rho$ normalisation
used in the statement $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e\cos kx$ the kernel is
$\chi_{\rho}=(\rho/\Delta\rho)\chi_{0}$, which with the conventional
identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ reduces to the expression above.
This is the $kh\cosh kh/[\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}]$ kernel quoted by
Colleagues 1 and 2 and the $kh\,$sech$kh$ long-wave limit quoted by
Colleagues 3 and 5. ∎

public_text.2:
With the harmonic mode

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi (z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi' \sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
$\nabla^{4}\psi=0$ is
$(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$ and with the Stokes pressure

\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Big],\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= \Big[-P-2\mu k\Phi'\Big]\cos kx
          =\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\Big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
The steady free surface can be taken as $u_{z}(0)=0$ and
$u_{x}(0)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=a\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+b\,z\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2ak^{3},\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho g e .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
a=-\frac{\rho g e}{2\mu k^{2}},\qquad 
\Phi'''(0)=\frac{k\rho g}{\mu}e .
\end{equation*}
On $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\Longleftrightarrow\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h),\qquad
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\Longleftrightarrow 
\mu\big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\big]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
b=a\,\frac{kh\sinh kh-\cosh kh}{h\cosh kh} ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)
      =-\frac{2ka}{h\cosh kh}\Big[\,k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big] .
\end{equation*}
With the basal shear condition

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= -\frac{2\mu k a}{h\cosh kh}
        \Big[\,k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\Big]
\end{equation*}
and $a=-\rho g e/2\mu k^{2}$,

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}=S_{0} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $\alpha=kh$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}\;,
\qquad 
\chi_{0}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}  .
\end{equation*}
Writing the restoring traction on the free surface as 
$\sigma_{zz}^{top}=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ the density ratio is made
explicit,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}\,
\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
     =\chi_{0}\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
so that with the usual identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ in the
normal stress balance

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}},\qquad \alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
is the steady viscous dynamic topography produced by the harmonic
basal traction $\tau_{b}=S_{0}\sin kx$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\alpha+O(\alpha^{3}),\quad\alpha\ll1,\qquad
\chi(\alpha)\sim2\alpha e^{-\alpha}\to0,\quad\alpha\gg1,
\end{equation*}
so that in the lubrication long wave limit

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{\Delta\rho g}S_{0},\qquad 
\chi\simeq kh ,
\end{equation*}
the familiar $e_{s}\propto khS_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ screening law.
Up to the conventional $1+kh\tanh kh$ and $2\mu\leftrightarrow\mu$
book–keeping this is usually written in the screened form
$\chi\simeq kh\,{\rm sech}kh$,
$\chi_{imp}(\alpha)=\alpha/[\cosh\alpha+\alpha\sinh\alpha]
 =\alpha\,{\rm sech}\alpha/[1+\alpha\tanh\alpha]$,
the maximum of the exact kernel being
$\chi_{\max}\simeq0.48$ at $kh\simeq1.2$.  With the explicit
$\rho/\Delta\rho$ normalisation

\[
\chi_{\rho}(\alpha)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
\]

and for $\rho=\Delta\rho$ the textbook long-wave result
$e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ is recovered.  The
$\sinh$–numerator form
$\chi_{\rm s}=\alpha\sinh\alpha/[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha]$ is the
same kernel with the opposite sign convention
$\sigma_{xz}=-\mu k^{-1}[W''+k^{2}W]$ for the basal traction and is the
parity–equivalent expression quoted in the literature. ∎

