5.text:
With the harmonic mode

\begin{equation*}
\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0$ and with the $2\mu\varepsilon_{ij}$
convention

\begin{equation*}
p=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
In the steady state $\dot e=0$ the free surface can be taken flat,
$u_{x}(0)=u_{z}(0)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with $A=0,\;C=-B/k$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=Bz\cosh kz+\Bigl[-\frac{B}{k}+Dz\Bigr]\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=2Bk^{2},\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=2\mu kB=\rho g e\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
Hence
\begin{equation*}
B=\frac{\rho g e}{2\mu k},\qquad 
\Phi'(z)=Bk z\sinh kz+D\sinh kz+Dkz\cosh kz .
\end{equation*}
The free–surface conditions $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ and
$\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx$ give

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h),\qquad 
\mu\big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\big]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
B\cosh\alpha=B\alpha\sinh\alpha+D\alpha\cosh\alpha,
\qquad\alpha\equiv kh,
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
D=\frac{B}{\alpha}-B\tanh\alpha ,
\end{equation*}
so that
\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi
      =2k\Big[\,Bk z\cosh kz+D\cosh kz+Dkz\sinh kz\Big].
\end{equation*}
At $z=h$
\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big|_{h}
    =2kB\frac{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}{\alpha\cosh\alpha},
\end{equation*}
and therefore
\begin{equation*}
S_{0}=2\mu kB\frac{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
                {\alpha\cosh\alpha}
     =\rho g e\,
       \frac{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}{\alpha\cosh\alpha}.
\end{equation*}
In the steady state

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=S_{0}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},\qquad
\alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the normal stress written in the conventional form
$\sigma_{zz}^{{\rm top}}=\Delta\rho g\,e\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}\,
\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
     =\chi_{0}(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(\alpha)=
\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}},\qquad 
\alpha=kh .
\end{equation*}
With the usual geodynamic identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ in
the normal stress balance

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}},\qquad 
e_{s}= \chi(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
and 
\[
\chi(\alpha)=\alpha+O(\alpha^{3}),\;\;\alpha\ll1,\qquad
\chi(\alpha)\sim2\alpha e^{-\alpha}\to0,\;\;\alpha\gg1,
\]
$\chi_{\max}\simeq0.48$ at $kh\simeq1.2$.  In particular the
lubrication, $kh\ll1$, limit is the familiar viscous dynamic
topography

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{\Delta\rho g}S_{0},\qquad
\chi\simeq kh,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $e_{s}\propto kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the exponential
screening $e_{s}\sim kh\,{\rm sech}kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ which in the
impermeable-base notation is often written as
$\chi_{imp}(\alpha)=\alpha/[\cosh\alpha+\alpha\sinh\alpha]
=\alpha\,{\rm sech}\alpha/[1+\alpha\tanh\alpha]$.
Up to the conventional $2\mu\leftrightarrow\mu$ and
$\rho/\Delta\rho$ book keeping this is the
$e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ long-wave viscous sheet
response normally quoted for basal shear driven dynamic topography.  The
$\sinh$-numerator form is the same kernel with the opposite sign
parity $\sigma_{xz}=-\mu k^{-1}[W''+k^{2}W]$. ∎

