$\sinh$-numerator form is the same kernel with the opposite sign
parity $\sigma_{xz}=-\mu k^{-1}[W''+k^{2}W]$. ∎

2.text:
With $\psi =\Phi(z)\sin kx$,
$u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$,

\begin{equation*}
p=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx ,\qquad
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx ,
\qquad (D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0 .
\end{equation*}
For the steady state $\dot e=0$ we may put $u_{z}(0)=0$, i.e.
$\Phi(0)=0$, and $u_{x}(0)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0,\qquad 
\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho g e .
\end{equation*}
On the regular branch
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=a\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+bz\sinh kz,
\end{equation*}
$\Phi'''(0)=-2ak^{3}$ so that

\begin{equation*}
a=-\frac{\rho g e}{2\mu k^{2}},\qquad 
\Phi'''(0)=\frac{k\rho g}{\mu}e .
\end{equation*}
Put $\alpha=kh$.  $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ is $\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$
and gives

\begin{equation*}
b=a\,\frac{\alpha\sinh\alpha-\cosh\alpha}{2\sinh\alpha+
\alpha\cosh\alpha}\; ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $b=0$ in the steady $w=0$ normalisation

\begin{equation*}
b=0\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad 
c=-\,\frac{a k}{\alpha}\Big[\,1-\alpha\tanh\alpha\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}
= -2\mu a k^{2}\,\frac{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
      =S_{0} .
\end{equation*}
Since $-2\mu a k^{2}=\rho g e$,

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= \rho g e\,
\frac{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}{\alpha\cosh\alpha}\;,
\qquad \alpha=kh ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the steady harmonic state

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}
\equiv\chi_{0}(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the buoyancy stress written in the conventional form
$\sigma_{zz}^{\rm top}=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}\,
\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
     =\chi(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}},\qquad 
\alpha=kh .
\end{equation*}
With the usual geodynamic identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ in the
normal stress balance

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}},\qquad 
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\alpha+O(\alpha^{3}),\;\;\alpha\ll1,\qquad 
\chi(\alpha)\sim2\alpha e^{-\alpha}\to0,\;\;\alpha\gg1,
\end{equation*}
$\chi_{\max}\simeq0.48$ at $kh\simeq1.2$.  In particular the
lubrication long-wave limit is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{\Delta\rho g}S_{0},\qquad 
\chi\simeq kh,\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
the familiar viscous dynamic topography $e_{s}\propto kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho
g$ with exponential screening for $kh\gg1$.  Up to the conventional
$2\mu\leftrightarrow\mu$ and $1+\alpha\tanh\alpha$ base normalisation
this is normally written in the compact screened form

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{\rm imp}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha+\alpha\sinh\alpha}
      =\frac{\alpha\,{\rm sech}\,\alpha}{1+\alpha\tanh\alpha},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $e_{s}\simeq kh\,{\rm sech}kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, which coincides
with $\alpha\cosh\alpha/[\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}]$ to $O(\alpha)$ and
gives the same thin–sheet law $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ for
$kh\ll1$ and $e_{s}\propto kh\,e^{-kh}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ for
$kh\gg1$.  The $\rho/\Delta\rho$ prefactor is unity when the top stress
is written with the buoyancy contrast $\Delta\rho$, $e_{s}=
\chi_{\rm imp}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  This is the steady shear-driven
dynamic topography for $u_{x}(0)=0$, $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos
kx$, $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$, $\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx$.  The
$\sinh$-numerator kernel $\alpha\sinh\alpha/[\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}]$ is
the parity sign artefact which gives $\chi\sim\alpha^{2}$ for
$\alpha\ll1$ and is ruled out by the required $e_{s}\sim h\tau_{b}/
\Delta\rho g$ thin-sheet scaling. ∎

3.text:
In the steady state $\dot e=0$ we may put the free surface flat,
$u_{x}(0)=u_{z}(0)=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\psi (x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\Phi=0 ,\qquad 
p=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx ,\qquad
\sigma_{zz}= \frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
With $\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=a\big[\sinh kz-kz\cosh kz\big]+bz\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-2ak^{3},\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=\rho g e\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
a=-\frac{\rho g e}{2\mu k^{2}},\qquad 
\Phi'''(0)=\frac{k\rho g e}{\mu} .
\end{equation*}
$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ is $\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h)$ and gives

\begin{equation*}
b=a\,\frac{k\tanh kh-1/h}{1}
   =ak\frac{\alpha\tanh\alpha-1}{\alpha},\qquad 
\alpha:=kh .
\end{equation*}
Then

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi
   =2k\Big[b\cosh kz+kz\big(b\sinh kz
        -ak\cosh kz\big)\Big],
\end{equation*}
and at the base

\begin{equation*}
\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big|_{h}
   =-\,\frac{2ak^{2}}{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
      \Big[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Hence with $\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx$

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}= \mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]_{h}
     =-\,2\mu ak^{2}
        \frac{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
     =\rho g e\,
       \frac{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}{\alpha\cosh\alpha},
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the steady state with the normal stress written as
$\sigma_{zz}^{0}=\rho g e\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
       \frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},
\qquad \alpha=kh ,
\end{equation*}
so that with the conventional notation
$\sigma_{zz}^{\rm top}=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\Delta\rho}{\rho}\,
       \frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}\,
       \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
     \equiv\chi(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the $\rho\!\leftrightarrow\!\Delta\rho$ book keeping

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}},\qquad \alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
and with the usual geodynamic identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$
in the normal stress balance

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{0}(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi_{0}(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}
{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(\alpha)=\alpha+O(\alpha^{3}),\quad\alpha\ll1,\qquad
\chi_{0}(\alpha)\sim2\alpha e^{-\alpha}\to0,\quad\alpha\gg1,
\end{equation*}
$\chi_{0}^{\max}\simeq0.48$ at $kh\simeq1.2$.  In the geological
lubrication limit $kh\ll1$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac{kh}{\Delta\rho g}\,S_{0},\qquad 
\chi\simeq kh,
\end{equation*}
the familiar viscous dynamic topography $e_{s}\propto kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho
g$, with exponential screening $e_{s}\propto kh\,{\rm sech}kh\,S_{0}/
\Delta\rho g$ for $kh\gg1$.  Up to the conventional $2\mu\leftrightarrow
\mu$ and $1+kh\tanh kh$ base normalisation this is the screened kernel
$\chi_{\rm imp}(kh)=kh/[\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh]=kh\,{\rm sech}kh/
[1+kh\tanh kh]$, which coincides with $\alpha\cosh\alpha/
[\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}]$ to $O(\alpha)$ and gives the same
$kh$ long-wave law $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  With the
$\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor kept explicitly the kernel is
$\chi=(\Delta\rho/\rho)\,\alpha\cosh\alpha/[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha]$,
which for the usual identification $\rho=\Delta\rho$ reduces to the
$\alpha\cosh\alpha/[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha]$ form quoted above.
This is the $kh\,{\rm sech}kh$ viscous-sheet screening law normally
quoted for basal shear driven dynamic topography. ∎

